# Long-form guides (replace the short versions with the same slug). '## ' starts an H2, '- ' lines become a list.
LONG={
'how-to-edit-wedding-album-psd':('How to Edit a Wedding Album PSD in Photoshop (Step-by-Step)',[
"A wedding album PSD template saves hours, but only if you know how it is built. This guide walks through editing a typical 12x36 album sheet in Adobe Photoshop, from opening the file to exporting a print-ready JPG, with the habits that keep album work fast and error-free.",
"## 1. Check the document before you touch anything",
"Open the PSD and press Ctrl+Alt+I (Image Size). Note the width, height and resolution. A 12x36 inch sheet at 300 DPI is 10800 x 3600 pixels; at 200 DPI it is 7200 x 2400. Compare this with what your lab asks for. If the lab wants 12x36 at 300 DPI and the file is smaller, decide now whether to work at the template's size or resize the canvas, because resizing after you place photos softens everything.",
"Next, turn on View > Rulers and drag a vertical guide to the exact centre (18 inches on a 36 inch sheet). That is the fold. Also add guides about 0.25 inch inside every edge to mark the safe area. Faces, names and dates must stay inside these guides.",
"## 2. Understand the layer structure",
"Good templates group their layers: photo frames, decorations, text and background. Open the Layers panel and expand the groups. A photo frame is usually a shape or a filled layer named something like 'Photo 1' or 'Place image here'. Your photo will be clipped into that shape, so it only shows inside the frame.",
"If a template is a single flattened layer, it is not really editable; pick a different design. Every file on PSD Album is layered, and the design page lists the canvas size, DPI and file size before you download.",
"## 3. Place and clip your photos",
"- Select the frame layer in the Layers panel.",
"- Use File > Place Embedded to bring in your photo. It lands directly above the selected layer.",
"- Press Alt+Ctrl+G (Option+Cmd+G on Mac) to create a clipping mask. The photo now shows only inside the frame.",
"- Press Ctrl+T, hold Shift if your version needs it, and scale the photo so the subject fills the frame without cutting off heads or hands.",
"Placing photos as Smart Objects (Place Embedded does this) means you can resize them many times without losing quality. Avoid scaling a photo above 100% of its original size; if a frame is too large for your photo, choose a different frame or a different photo.",
"## 4. Edit text without breaking the layout",
"Double-click the T thumbnail of a text layer to select its text and type the couple's names, the date and the venue. If the template uses a font you do not have, Photoshop shows a warning; replace it with a similar font from your system and keep one font pair across the whole album. Check every spelling with the client; names and dates are the first thing families notice.",
"## 5. Recolour decorations safely",
"Instead of painting on the original ornaments, add a Hue/Saturation or Color Balance adjustment layer and clip it to the decoration layer or group. This lets you shift gold to rose gold, or maroon to teal, and undo it at any time. Keep the palette consistent across all spreads of the album.",
"## 6. Proof the spread",
"Zoom to 100% and scroll across the whole sheet. Look for photos that do not fully cover their frame, soft or noisy images, text too close to the fold and stray layers left visible. Turn off any 'guide' or 'sample text' layers the template designer left in the file.",
"## 7. Export for print",
"Save the layered PSD first (File > Save As) so you can make client changes later. Then use File > Save a Copy and choose JPEG at quality 10 to 12, or the format your lab asks for. Most Indian digital labs print from sRGB JPG files; convert with Edit > Convert to Profile if your file is in another profile. Name files in page order (01.jpg, 02.jpg …) so the lab binds the album correctly.",
"## Common mistakes to avoid",
"- Resizing the canvas after placing photos, which blurs everything.",
"- Leaving faces on the centre fold, where the binding swallows them.",
"- Using five different fonts in one album.",
"- Sending CMYK files to a lab that expects RGB, which dulls colours.",
"With a clean workflow, a 30-sheet album built from templates takes a fraction of the time of designing from scratch, and the quality stays consistent from the first spread to the last."]),
'12x36-album-size-guide':('12x36 Album Size Guide: Pixels, DPI and Print Settings',[
"12x36 inches is the most popular wedding album spread size in India. When the book is closed each page is 12x18 inches; opened flat, the two pages form one 12x36 panoramic sheet. This guide explains the pixel sizes, DPI choices and print settings that matter when you design or edit a 12x36 album.",
"## Pixel dimensions at common resolutions",
"Pixels are simply inches multiplied by DPI (dots per inch):",
"- 300 DPI: 10800 x 3600 pixels. The safest choice for sharp prints.",
"- 250 DPI: 9000 x 3000 pixels.",
"- 200 DPI: 7200 x 2400 pixels. Accepted by many labs for photo-printed albums.",
"- 150 DPI: 5400 x 1800 pixels. Lowest that most labs will accept; fine detail may soften.",
"Every design page on PSD Album shows the exact pixel size and DPI of the file, so you can calculate the print size before downloading: divide pixels by DPI. For example, 10800 / 300 = 36 inches.",
"## Which DPI should you use?",
"Albums are viewed at arm's length, so 300 DPI is generous and 200 to 250 DPI is usually indistinguishable in print. The real limit is your photos: a 24-megapixel camera image is about 6000 x 4000 pixels, which can cover a 20 x 13 inch area at 300 DPI. If you place a cropped phone photo into a large frame, it will look soft no matter what DPI the template uses. Higher DPI also means bigger files; a layered 12x36 PSD at 300 DPI can be several hundred megabytes, so make sure your computer has enough RAM (16 GB is comfortable).",
"## Bleed, trim and the centre fold",
"Labs trim a little from every edge after printing, so keep at least 0.25 inch (75 pixels at 300 DPI) of 'bleed' where background artwork continues but nothing important sits. Keep faces and text a further 0.25 inch inside that. The fold at 18 inches is the most dangerous place on the sheet; in lay-flat albums it is mostly visible, but in thicker books the binding hides a small strip. Never put a face or a name across the fold.",
"## Colour settings",
"Most Indian digital labs print photo albums on photographic paper using RGB workflows and want sRGB JPG files. Do not convert to CMYK unless the lab specifically asks; CMYK conversion often makes reds, maroons and golds look flat. Ask whether the lab applies its own colour correction, and if you have a calibrated monitor, soft-proof with the lab's profile.",
"## 12x36 vs other sizes",
"- 12x30: a slightly shorter panorama (9000 x 3600 px at 300 DPI) that many labs print at lower cost.",
"- 12x18: compact sheets and covers; also the closed size of a 12x36 album.",
"- 18x24: large-format premium albums with fewer, bigger photos.",
"- 14x40: an even wider panorama some premium labs offer; check that your templates are built for it rather than stretching a 12x36 file.",
"## Export checklist",
"- Image Size shows the size your lab asked for.",
"- Colour profile is sRGB unless the lab says otherwise.",
"- No faces or text in the bleed or across the fold.",
"- Flattened JPG at maximum quality, named in page order.",
"- Layered PSD archived for reprints.",
"Getting the size right at the start is the single biggest time saver in album work. Browse our 12x36 wedding album PSD collection to find sheets already set up at the correct proportions."]),
'karizma-album-design-tips':('Karizma Album Design Tips for Photo Studios',[
"'Karizma album' is the name Indian studios commonly use for premium photo-printed albums: thick laminated pages printed on photographic paper, usually in a 12x36 spread. Clients pay more for them, so the design has to look premium from the cover to the last page. These are practical tips that experienced album designers use.",
"## Plan the story before opening Photoshop",
"Sort the selected photos into the order of the day: preparations, the ceremony, rituals, couple portraits, family groups and the reception. Decide how many sheets each part gets. A typical Karizma album has 30 to 40 sheets; giving 6 sheets to haldi and 2 to the reception will feel unbalanced. A written page plan also makes client approval much faster.",
"## One hero photo per spread",
"The most common mistake is filling every frame. Choose one strong photo for each spread, make it large, and support it with two to four smaller images that add context such as details, reactions and decor. Empty space is not wasted space; it is what makes a layout look expensive.",
"## Use templates as a starting grid, not a cage",
"Templates give you balanced frame positions, shadows and ornaments. Hide decorations that fight with your photos, move frames to suit the orientation of your images, and delete frames you cannot fill with good pictures. A template with half its ornaments hidden often looks more premium than the original.",
"## Keep a consistent palette",
"Pick two or three colours from the wedding itself (the bride's saree, the mandap decor) and use them across the album. Recolour template ornaments with clipped Hue/Saturation adjustment layers instead of mixing gold, silver, red and blue designs in one book. Use backgrounds from the same set for an entire event.",
"## Typography",
"Use one decorative font for names and headings and one clean font for small text. Keep names large on the opening spread and the cover, and avoid long quotes in tiny script fonts; they are hard to read on glossy laminated pages.",
"## Respect the fold and the lamination",
"Karizma pages are thick, and lamination can add glare, so keep faces at least half an inch from the centre fold and avoid placing text over busy textures. Check the lab's bleed requirement and keep important elements inside the safe area.",
"## Colour and sharpness",
"Colour-correct all photos of an event together so skin tones match from page to page. Do a final sharpen for print at the output size, not before resizing. Proof at 100% zoom; photo-printed albums show softness and noise more than you expect.",
"## Speed up the workflow",
"- Save your favourite frame layouts as your own PSD templates.",
"- Use actions to place, clip and resize photos.",
"- Batch-export finished sheets with numbered file names.",
"- Keep a client approval sheet: a low-resolution JPG preview of every spread.",
"## Where to find layouts",
"Our Karizma album PSD collection includes royal, floral, golden and minimal 12x36 sheets, each with a full preview and specs on its own page, free to download without a password."]),
'print-ready-psd-checklist':('Print-Ready PSD Checklist for Albums and Covers',[
"A beautiful spread can still come back from the lab with cut-off names, dull colours or blurry faces. Most of these problems are avoidable with a quick check before export. Use this checklist for every album sheet, cover and invitation you send to print.",
"## Size and resolution",
"- Open Image > Image Size and confirm the print size in inches matches the lab's order form (for example 36 x 12 in for a 12x36 sheet).",
"- Resolution between 200 and 300 DPI unless the lab specifies otherwise.",
"- No photo placed above 100% of its original pixel size; check Smart Object scale in the Properties panel.",
"## Safe area, bleed and fold",
"- Background artwork extends to the edge of the canvas (bleed).",
"- Faces, names and dates are at least 0.25 to 0.5 inch inside the trim line.",
"- Nothing important crosses the centre fold of a spread.",
"- For covers, the lab's wrap-around template is used, with the correct spine width.",
"## Colour",
"- Document colour profile is sRGB (or exactly what the lab requests).",
"- Skin tones look consistent across facing pages and across the album.",
"- Recolouring was done with adjustment layers, and no accidental adjustment layer is affecting the whole sheet.",
"- If you edited on a very bright laptop screen, check brightness on a second screen or a test print; most album complaints are prints that are too dark.",
"## Text",
"- All names, dates and venues have been confirmed in writing with the client.",
"- No placeholder text such as 'Your Name Here' or Lorem ipsum remains.",
"- Fonts are installed or text is rasterised in the print copy, so the lab cannot substitute fonts.",
"- Small text is not placed over busy textures.",
"## Layers and housekeeping",
"- Template guide layers, watermarks and hidden sample photos are deleted, not only hidden.",
"- Layered PSD saved and backed up for corrections and reprints.",
"- Flattened print copy saved as JPG (quality 10 to 12) or TIFF as the lab prefers.",
"## File naming and delivery",
"- Files numbered in page order: 01_cover.jpg, 02.jpg, 03.jpg …",
"- A low-resolution preview PDF or contact sheet sent to the client for approval before printing.",
"- Files uploaded to the lab in one folder with the order number in the folder name.",
"## A final 60-second check",
"Zoom to 100%, press Tab to hide panels and scroll the whole sheet once from left to right. Then zoom out to fit the screen and look at it like the client will: does the eye land on the hero photo first? If yes, export."]),
'album-cover-dimensions':('Album Cover Dimensions: Sizes for 12x36, 12x30 and 12x18 Albums',[
"Album cover files confuse many new designers because the cover is not the same size as an inside spread. This guide explains how cover sizes relate to album sizes and what to ask your lab before you design.",
"## The closed size is half the spread",
"A 12x36 album opens to a 12x36 inch spread, so the closed book (and the front cover) is 12 inches tall and 18 inches wide, landscape. A 12x30 album closes to 12x15. A 12x18 spread album closes to 12x9, and so on. Start any cover design from the closed size.",
"## Front-only covers vs wrap-around covers",
"Some labs print only a front panel that is fixed onto a padded or acrylic cover. In that case the file is simply the closed size plus the lab's bleed, for example 12.25 x 18.25 inches for a 12x18 front with 0.125 inch bleed on each side.",
"Other labs print a full wrap: back cover + spine + front cover + extra margin that folds around the board. The width is roughly 2 x closed width + spine width + 2 x wrap margin. Spine width depends on the number of sheets and paper thickness, so it changes from album to album. Always download the lab's cover template for the exact sheet count.",
"## Pixel sizes at 300 DPI",
"- 12x18 front: 5400 x 3600 pixels.",
"- 12x15 front: 4500 x 3600 pixels.",
"- 12x24 front (for 12x48 spreads): 7200 x 3600 pixels.",
"Add bleed and wrap margins to these numbers as your lab requires.",
"## Design tips",
"- Use one strong couple photo; covers with many small photos look like the inside pages.",
"- Keep names and the date large and centred on the front panel, well inside the safe area.",
"- On wrap-around covers, keep the spine simple: names or initials only, centred.",
"- Avoid fine script fonts on textured or leather-look covers; they lose detail.",
"- Match the cover palette to the album's opening spread.",
"## Using a cover PSD from this site",
"Our album cover PSDs are designed as front panels. Check the pixel size on the design page, place it on the front panel of your lab's template, and extend the background into the bleed. Then add the spine and back cover from the same colour palette."]),
'how-to-download-psd-files':('How to Download PSD Files from PSD Album (and Fix Common Problems)',[
"Every template on PSD Album is free and does not need a login or a password. Files are delivered from Google Drive. This page explains the download steps and how to solve the problems people most often write to us about.",
"## Step by step",
"- Open a design page and check the preview, size, DPI and file size.",
"- Click 'Download PSD — Free'. A download page opens and prepares your Google Drive link for a few seconds.",
"- Click 'Download from Google Drive' when it appears. Drive opens in a new tab.",
"- Large files cannot be virus-scanned by Google, so Drive shows a notice. Choose 'Download anyway'.",
"- Your browser saves a ZIP file. Extract it and open the PSD in Adobe Photoshop CC.",
"## Drive says 'Too many users have viewed or downloaded this file'",
"Popular files sometimes hit Google's temporary download quota. Wait a few hours and try again, or sign in to a Google account, open the file, and use 'Add shortcut to Drive' or 'Make a copy', then download from your own Drive. If it persists for more than a day, email us with the design ID.",
"## The ZIP will not open or says it is damaged",
"This almost always means the download did not finish. Check that the file size on your computer matches the size listed on the design page. Download again on a stable connection, preferably with a desktop browser rather than a mobile data hotspot. Use 7-Zip on Windows or The Unarchiver on Mac for very large ZIPs. Our guide on fixing ZIP extraction errors has more detail.",
"## Photoshop shows missing fonts",
"The template uses a font that is not installed on your computer. Photoshop will substitute a default font when you edit the text. You can replace it with any similar font you own; keep one font family across the album for consistency.",
"## The file is very large or Photoshop is slow",
"Album sheets at 300 DPI can be several hundred megabytes. Close other programs, give Photoshop more memory in Preferences > Performance, and set a scratch disk on a drive with plenty of free space. Saving as PSB is only needed for files larger than 2 GB.",
"## Can I use the files for clients?",
"Yes. You can use templates for personal projects and for client work such as albums you design for customers. Please do not re-upload or resell the PSD files themselves. See our Terms of Use.",
"## Still stuck?",
"Email contact@psdalbum.in with the design ID (shown as #psd-1234 on the design page) and a short description. We usually fix broken links within a day or two."]),
'how-to-change-studio-background-in-photoshop':('How to Change a Studio Background in Photoshop',[
"Replacing a plain or messy background with a clean studio backdrop is one of the most common jobs in a photo studio, from passport photos to kids portraits and festival prints. With modern Photoshop it takes a few minutes. Here is a reliable method.",
"## 1. Open the photo and the backdrop",
"Open your portrait and a studio background PSD. Check that the background is at least as large as your final print; a 12x18 print at 300 DPI needs 5400 x 3600 pixels.",
"## 2. Select the subject",
"In the portrait, choose Select > Subject. Photoshop's AI selection handles most people well. Then open Select > Select and Mask. Use the Refine Hair button or the Refine Edge Brush over hair and fabric edges. Set Output To: New Layer with Layer Mask and click OK.",
"## 3. Move the subject onto the backdrop",
"Drag the masked layer into the background document. Press Ctrl+T to scale and position the subject. Keep feet or the bottom edge consistent with the floor line of the backdrop so the person does not look like they are floating.",
"## 4. Match light and colour",
"This is what separates a believable result from an obvious cut-out:",
"- Add a Curves adjustment layer clipped to the subject (Alt+Ctrl+G) and match brightness and contrast to the backdrop.",
"- Use a clipped Color Balance or Photo Filter layer to match warmth. A warm golden backdrop with a cool blue subject never looks right.",
"- Check the light direction. If the backdrop has light from the left, the subject's highlights should be on the left too; flip the backdrop horizontally if needed.",
"## 5. Add a soft shadow",
"Create a new layer below the subject, paint a soft black shadow under the feet or behind the shoulders with a large soft brush at low opacity, and lower the layer opacity until it looks natural. Contact shadows are what make the subject 'sit' in the scene.",
"## 6. Clean up edges",
"Zoom to 100% and look for halos of the old background around hair and shoulders. Paint on the layer mask with a soft black brush to remove them, or use Layer > Matting > Defringe on a duplicated, merged copy.",
"## 7. Export",
"Flatten a copy and export as JPG in sRGB for printing or sharing. Keep the layered file in case the client wants a different backdrop; with the subject masked you can swap backgrounds in seconds.",
"## Tips for faster studio work",
"- Shoot against a plain, evenly lit wall to make selections cleaner.",
"- Keep a folder of favourite backdrops sorted by colour and mood.",
"- Record an action for the Curves and Color Balance layers you use most often."]),
'rgb-vs-cmyk-for-albums':('RGB vs CMYK for Photo Albums: What Indian Labs Expect',[
"Should an album file be RGB or CMYK? It is one of the most common questions from new album designers, and the wrong choice can make maroons, golds and skin tones look dull. The short answer: for most photo-printed albums in India, send sRGB files unless your lab tells you otherwise.",
"## How albums are printed",
"Photo-printed albums, including Karizma and lay-flat albums, are usually printed on photographic paper with digital minilabs or wide-format photo printers. These machines take RGB input and convert it internally using profiles tuned for the paper. Sending them a CMYK file forces an extra conversion and often loses the brightest reds, greens and blues.",
"Offset and press-printed products, such as printed invitation cards and brochures, are different. Commercial presses do print with CMYK inks, and printers may ask for CMYK PDFs with a specific profile.",
"## Why CMYK looks dull",
"The CMYK colour range is smaller than RGB. When you convert, colours outside that range are pulled in, so bright saturated tones become flatter. Wedding albums are full of saturated sarees, gold jewellery and red decor, so the difference is easy to see.",
"## Which RGB profile?",
"sRGB is the safest choice: it is what most labs, screens and phones expect. Adobe RGB has a wider range, but if the lab's workflow ignores the embedded profile, Adobe RGB files print dull and slightly off. Use sRGB unless your lab confirms it handles Adobe RGB. In Photoshop, check with Edit > Convert to Profile and choose sRGB IEC61966-2.1.",
"## Practical workflow",
"- Edit photos and album sheets in sRGB, or in Adobe RGB and convert to sRGB at export.",
"- Do not convert album sheets to CMYK unless the lab requests it in writing.",
"- Ask whether the lab applies automatic colour correction; some do, which can undo careful edits. Many labs offer a 'no correction' option.",
"- For printed invitation cards, ask the printer for its CMYK profile and file format, and soft-proof before converting.",
"## Monitor brightness matters more",
"Most 'colour' complaints about albums are actually brightness problems: designers edit on a bright laptop screen and the print comes out dark. Lower your screen brightness when editing for print, use a calibrated monitor if you can, and order a test print of one sheet before printing a whole album.",
"## Summary",
"Photo albums: sRGB JPG. Press-printed cards: CMYK as specified by the printer. When in doubt, ask the lab and keep your layered RGB master file."]),
'birthday-album-psd-ideas':('Birthday and Baby Album Ideas Clients Love',[
"Birthday and baby albums are a big share of studio work after weddings, and they need a very different style: brighter, softer and more playful. Here are layout and story ideas that make these albums feel personal, plus tips for using birthday album PSD templates.",
"## Build the album as a story",
"- Opening spread: the child's name, age and a hero portrait.",
"- Preparations: decorations, the cake, the venue being set up.",
"- Arrival and dressing: the birthday outfit and close-up details.",
"- Cake cutting: a sequence of three to five photos across one spread.",
"- Games and friends: candid moments with cousins and friends.",
"- Family: parents, grandparents and the extended family group.",
"- Closing page: a thank-you note or a quiet portrait.",
"## First birthday milestone pages",
"For first birthdays, many parents love a 'twelve months' spread with one photo from each month, along with the baby's weight, first word or first steps. Templates with a grid of small frames work well for this; keep the background simple so twelve photos do not feel busy.",
"## Colour and theme",
"Match the album to the party theme: jungle, princess, cartoon, space or a pastel colour scheme. Pick two or three colours from the decorations and recolour template ornaments with adjustment layers so every spread uses the same palette. Avoid mixing many different template styles in one album.",
"## Text that adds meaning",
"Short captions such as the date, the venue, 'First steps', or a line from the parents make the album more personal than decorative quotes. Use one playful font for headings and one clean font for small text.",
"## Baby and newborn albums",
"Newborn albums need softer tones (cream, pastel pink, sky blue, mint) and larger photos with plenty of white space. Include details such as tiny hands and feet, the naming ceremony and the first family portrait. Baby shower spreads can lead into the newborn section to tell the whole story.",
"## Working with templates",
"Choose birthday album PSDs where the frames match the orientation of your best photos, and hide ornaments that compete with the child's face. Keep faces away from the centre fold on 12x36 sheets, and check spelling of the child's name on every page. Browse our birthday album PSD collection for free 12x36 kids and first birthday designs."]),
'how-to-make-wedding-album-in-photoshop':('How to Make a Wedding Album in Photoshop: A Complete Workflow',[
"Designing a full wedding album is a project, not a single edit. A typical album has 30 to 40 spreads and hundreds of photos, so a clear workflow matters more than any single Photoshop trick. This guide covers the full process studios use, from photo selection to delivery.",
"## 1. Collect requirements",
"Before designing, confirm with the client and the lab: album size (12x36 is most common), number of sheets, paper type, cover style, and whether the lab needs files at 200 or 300 DPI. Ask the couple for must-include photos, names and spellings, and dates of each event.",
"## 2. Select and sort photos",
"Cull the photos to the best 8 to 12 per spread, and sort them into folders by event: preparation, ceremony, rituals, couple portraits, family, reception. Colour-correct each event as a batch in Lightroom or Camera Raw so skin tones match across pages. Export at full resolution in sRGB.",
"## 3. Make a page plan",
"Write a simple list: sheet 1 opening, sheets 2 to 5 bride's preparation, sheets 6 to 8 groom, and so on. Decide which spreads get a single hero photo and which are collages. This plan becomes your client approval document and keeps the album balanced.",
"## 4. Choose a template family",
"Pick templates from one style (royal, floral, minimal) so the album feels consistent. On PSD Album, use the category and tag chips to find layouts at your exact sheet size, and check the pixel size and DPI on the design page before downloading.",
"## 5. Design spread by spread",
"- Open the template, add a centre-fold guide and safe-area guides.",
"- Place photos with File > Place Embedded and clip them into frames with Alt+Ctrl+G.",
"- Resize with Ctrl+T, keeping faces away from the fold.",
"- Edit names and captions, keeping one font pair across the album.",
"- Recolour ornaments with clipped adjustment layers to match the wedding colours.",
"Save each spread as a numbered PSD (01.psd, 02.psd …) in one folder.",
"## 6. Client review",
"Export small JPG previews of every spread and share them as a PDF or a private gallery. Collect all changes in one round where possible. Mark each change on the page plan and update the spreads.",
"## 7. Final export",
"Run through a print checklist: correct size, sRGB, no text in the bleed, no placeholder text, spellings confirmed. Export flattened JPGs at maximum quality, named in page order, and keep the layered PSDs backed up for reprints.",
"## 8. Delivery and archiving",
"Upload to the lab with the order number in the folder name. After delivery, archive the project folder (photos, PSDs, exports and page plan) to an external drive or cloud storage. Couples often order parent albums or reprints months later.",
"## Time-saving habits",
"- Build a personal library of favourite layouts and save them as templates.",
"- Record Photoshop actions for placing and clipping photos.",
"- Keep a standard checklist and page plan document for every client.",
"Templates handle the layout; your selection, story and attention to detail are what clients pay for."]),
}
import glob as _g
for _f in sorted(_g.glob('guides_[xy]*.py')): exec(open(_f).read())
def apply_long(BLOG):
  have={s for s,_,_ in BLOG}
  lo=[(s,)+LONG[s] for s in LONG]  # long-form guides first (index, footer, about box)
  return lo+[x for x in BLOG if x[0] not in LONG]
